{"step_index":1754,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T06:10:54Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nProject: Build a DSA Learning Library Inside My Existing Code Flow Visualizer\n\n1. Your Role\n\nAct as a senior software engineer, DSA instructor, and educational UI/UX designer.\n\nI already have an application called \"Teach Me This Code\". It has a code editor, step-by-step execution visualization, variable tracking, teaching explanations, animations, and navigation controls.\n\nI want to extend this application into a beginner-friendly DSA learning platform.\n\nMy goal is to learn Data Structures and Algorithms from the fundamentals, practice curated LeetCode problems, understand solution patterns, visualize Python execution, and track my progress.\n\n2. Non-Negotiable Requirements\n\nDo not rebuild my application from scratch.\n\nDo not replace or rewrite my existing execution engine.\n\nDo not modify the original Python code used by the existing visualizer.\n\nPreserve all existing working features, animations, navigation, and explanations.\n\nDo not introduce OpenAI, Gemini, Claude, or any other AI APIs.\n\nDo not require API keys, paid services, cloud databases, authentication, or mandatory internet access.\n\nUse Python as the only supported programming language initially.\n\nStore learning progress locally on my computer.\n\nReuse existing components, dependencies, and architecture wherever possible.\n\nInspect the project before making changes.\n\nMake incremental changes and test them before proceeding.\n\n3. Phase 1: Create the DSA Learning Dashboard\n\nAdd a new navigation section called \"DSA Learning\".\n\nBuild a clean, beginner-friendly dashboard that matches the existing application's design.\n\nInclude:\n\nTotal problems in the curated library.\n\nProblems solved.\n\nProblems attempted.\n\nProblems not started.\n\nBookmarked problems.\n\nTopic-wise progress.\n\nDifficulty-wise progress.\n\nContinue Learning.\n\nRecently practiced problems.\n\nA beginner learning roadmap.\n\nUse actual saved data for progress statistics. Do not generate fake completion numbers or streaks.\n\nInitially include 300 curated, distinct DSA problems. These must be real, identifiable problems, not 300 generated titles or duplicated entries.\n\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-10-27T11:40:54+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
