d, each showing the solved/total ratio with respective progress bars. A prominent \"Continue Learning\" card will suggest continuing a recent problem or, if none, recommend a beginner problem with a \"Start Problem\" button for immediate visualization. A recent activity table or card list will display practiced problems, including title, topic, difficulty, status, and timestamp.\n\n\nThe beginner roadmap necessitates a structured learning path with sequential phases, each containing learning objectives, milestones, and relevant problems. Implementing this will enable users to advance through core concepts systematically, with direct \"Solve\" actions for each problem. The curated problem explorer will offer filter options to aid problem discovery. Bookmarking and status indicators will enable users to track and manage progress.\n\n\nA dedicated manager for DSA learning will handle data synchronization with local storage, managing user progress and displaying problem data. The problem loading function will populate the visualizer with problem details and starter code, marking the problem as attempted.  The overall architecture includes data fetching from a JavaScript file containing the problem library.\n\n\nThe existing test suite will be reviewed to ensure the new learning dashboard integrates without breaking any current functionality or tests related to the visualizer's execution engine. The plan involves adding the dashboard while keeping the engine, explainer, and existing logic untouched, ensuring all tests continue to pass. The comprehensive list of 300 LeetCode problems will be compiled, including problem numbers, titles, difficulties, topics, patterns, and Python solutions.\n\n\nA comprehensive list of data structures and algorithms (DSA) problems is being compiled, categorizing them by solution approaches such as Hash Sets, Frequency Arrays, Prefix Sum techniques, and Two Pointers. The list includes easy, medium, and hard problems, linking each to relevant