oblems are being investigated, focusing on connected components, BFS/DFS applications, and graph cloning with hash map-based node copying.\n\n\nConsidering graph traversal and connectivity problems. Breadth-First Search (BFS) and Depth-First Search (DFS) are being examined for reachability, cycle detection, and component identification. Multi-source BFS is being analyzed for shortest path calculations.\n\nDisjoint Set Union (DSU) or Union-Find is under evaluation for cycle detection and connected component analysis in undirected graphs. Topological sorting is being considered for dependency-based scheduling problems. Cycle detection is critical in graph scenarios.\n\nSpecific algorithms like reverse BFS/DFS, boundary DFS inversion, and 3-state coloring for cycle detection are being considered. Island area calculation and flood fill problems are analyzed for implementation strategies. \n\n\nExamining Dijkstra's algorithm applications, including min-heap utilization for shortest path problems and Prim/Kruskal for minimum spanning trees. Considering advanced graph algorithms: Eulerian paths, modified Dijkstra for varying constraints, topological sort for dependency graphs, and Bellman-Ford for shortest paths with constraints. Exploring diverse graph problem techniques: 2D BFS, critical connection identification, graph coloring, and memoized DFS for rich data analysis.\n\nAnalyzing dynamic programming problems, specifically exploring the Fibonacci transition pattern and bottom-up DP for optimal substructure scenarios. Considering recurrence relations for house robber problems with and without circular array considerations. Reviewing palindromic substring problems with center expansion and 2D DP strategies.\n\n\nConsidering dynamic programming techniques. Exploring 2-Digit Valid Jump DP, Coin Change, Maximum Product Subarray and others for implementation. Analyzing problems like Unique Paths and Longest Common Subsequence using 2D grid DP and character matching DP, respectively.\n\n\nDynamic Programming problems are being classified based on state machine approaches, including buy/sell stock, subset sums, and string interleaving.  Matrix traversal, sequence alignment, edit distance, and interval problems are being assessed for suitability. Regular expression matching and wildcard matching techniques are under consideration.\n\nGreedy algorithm problems are being explored, considering jump games, gas station scenarios, and grouping strategies. Partitioning label problems and parenthesis string validation are being reviewed.\n\n\nThe analysis now focuses on core algorithm problem categorization to refine the learning path. Specific techniques like greedy approaches, sorting for interval problems, and stack-based simulations are under consideration. The structure involves reviewing LeetCode problems, using examples from adjacent zero checks to custom comparator applications. The interval problems require sorting intervals for merge operations and overlap checks.\n\n\nInterval intersection problems are solved by checking if the maximum of the starting points is less than or equal to the minimum of the ending points. A math and geometry category is added, encompassing various problems solved using different mathematical techniques and geometric insights.  Bit manipulation problems are categorized, addressing how binary operations can be applied to solve diverse algorithm challenges.\n\n\nA problem count is being verified, aiming for a curated set of 300 distinct LeetCode problems. Each problem will have an ID, name, difficulty, topic, pattern, description, and runnable Python code.  Progress will be tracked using local storage with \"solved,\" \"attempted,\" \"bookmarked,\" \"history,\" and \"notes\" fields.\n\n\nInitializes the beginner roadmap with eight structured phases, each detailing learning objectives and relevant problems. The roadmap phases cover topics from fundamental data structures to advanced algorithms and will offer \"Solve\" actions for interactive learning. A progress display will indicate completed problems within each phase.\n\n\nA dashboard structure is proposed, organizing the content into header statistics, progress cards for topics and difficulties, and beginner roadmap with a problem explorer. The main layout encompasses a stats strip, \"Continue Learning\" and \"Recently Practiced\" cards, a grid of topic cards, a beginner roadmap timeline, and a curated problem library. The library will provide search, filter, and sorting functionalities for the problem catalog.\n\n\nClicking the \"Solve & Visualize in Python\" button triggers a view switch to the code visualizer. The code editor is updated with the problem's Python code and the top bar text. Problem data is saved in local storage. The `app.runUserCode()` function executes, initiating the visualization process. Returning to the DSA library is handled through specific UI elements, refreshing the dashboard progress.\n\n\nThe core problem library (`dsa_problems.js`) containing 300 curated DSA problems will be created with detailed metadata per problem. The `dsa_dashboard.js` module will manage the dashboard's internal state, integrate local storage for persistent user progress, and implement dashboard functionality including roadmap rendering and problem interaction.\n\nThe `index.html` file will be updated to include the necessary JavaScript files and add a view toggle between the visualizer and DSA learning dashboard sections. Modern CSS styles are being written for the DSA dashboard. The `app.js` file will provide integration by loading problems into the visualizer from the dashboard and managing view switching.\n\nFinally, verification will ensure the library accurately contains all 300 problems, and each problem's LeetCode number is valid.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"node -e 'console.log(\"Node is ready\")'","Cwd":"C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer","Is